[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d39e1d2-809f-5155-a07c-2be82fe6f5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339382} {:answer/id #uuid "40729786-50b6-5ff5-b256-a96bf88930f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339704} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3fcfc4-5970-535a-9851-ae25c946b062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{14})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340688} {:answer/id #uuid "dc526537-6440-5beb-89ba-a669341bd361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341086} {:answer/id #uuid "f54d10f1-3bdb-508e-83f2-6340ec8fe28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc526537-6440-5beb-89ba-a669341bd361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341086} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bdfe7ee-6a1b-5a6c-8716-9c970e741c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "188c3216-6730-58aa-bfa7-8985bab845cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-7305" :question/problem "Express $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11bc78-d2c8-508a-b8fb-990c82485b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339511} {:answer/id #uuid "672f2c27-138f-5c55-aef5-0b2361a5154e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-2+\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340041} {:answer/id #uuid "ab869e72-931c-52bc-97fc-2e5c42e4486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} {:answer/id #uuid "ee951d44-ea61-556b-94d7-e1dd4325b6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341232} {:answer/id #uuid "eee0bce5-f344-5783-ba0f-f51aa7490a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(\\frac{j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee951d44-ea61-556b-94d7-e1dd4325b6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341232} :answer-field/id #uuid "a983d74d-4019-5c4d-aa85-405465fd5a9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f9a39ce-97fb-5b82-8335-1f0d956000c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73160" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "331c20ed-c9a8-5ebf-bb07-3fd9c7d6644e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339581} {:answer/id #uuid "4a710490-742e-5db0-89ff-943d4738e568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339798} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc24dec-2575-5162-950f-37eb4515063e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340252} {:answer/id #uuid "7e805222-a218-5e1b-a01f-94c7dbe62184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340269} {:answer/id #uuid "cdddff64-4e91-5e54-8e48-28c9f91c2f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdddff64-4e91-5e54-8e48-28c9f91c2f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340957} :answer-field/id #uuid "006a7b5c-6ec0-5b8c-8b5a-b754a7de6392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6144fe4d-1170-577e-833f-34f148107f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73192" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ between $x=1$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1beaf82b-ca6d-5426-aa13-fd5815d423b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339368} {:answer/id #uuid "82061933-a612-5dfb-af1d-c4e789553102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340298} {:answer/id #uuid "8d319e2e-bf9d-56aa-8999-7345e8362465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340395} {:answer/id #uuid "c7389d59-06bc-5dbe-9262-9447a8c813ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340894} {:answer/id #uuid "f480be00-0128-5082-93a6-d17b5a45580c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82061933-a612-5dfb-af1d-c4e789553102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340298} :answer-field/id #uuid "6548d467-5048-50dd-98ac-f013e5313bb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41bab868-1060-5798-adf0-32f97366917b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73286" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090a45ae-4a14-53bd-bff9-96bc2c7a0d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339206} {:answer/id #uuid "4c52d9ff-6ce1-5908-b527-e5d1742ab70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339819} {:answer/id #uuid "5588d6f2-8c6f-5a01-b89a-7b7a17265739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339912} {:answer/id #uuid "d3238de0-22ee-5721-a21d-7e7f9b4dbeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340998} {:answer/id #uuid "e96c033c-24e5-59d6-9c28-291c33500939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e96c033c-24e5-59d6-9c28-291c33500939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341187} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da3650a-fb35-5fa8-b4ff-0230c249cc80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98dd252e-cda8-57e3-b4c8-968d1e2d356e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73289" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25da2f97-1233-53a9-916d-a71898b3df50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339455} {:answer/id #uuid "72f61544-e2c8-53c1-8a4e-3af5ff8bdb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340148} {:answer/id #uuid "adab89e2-8e27-5382-8960-921d0ee9c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340709} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d86727-c7e5-55c4-bf76-09b8a18150e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340879} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5f479-65eb-52bf-9ade-027e7bebf59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25da2f97-1233-53a9-916d-a71898b3df50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339455} :answer-field/id #uuid "979df9b1-6818-520d-9541-8aa235a34437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9cc7964b-3169-5f22-8d75-6a7a05d33c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73324" :question/problem "![](images/33/333a99cd7b1f4aa0c3017c2b5aa42cb04acad842b19345a1176d57a71c5fd120.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-{sec}^{2}⁡x$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, as shown above. Find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b55e79a-993d-5794-b926-9f61e9280aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339227} {:answer/id #uuid "35e5d8c3-75ae-5d03-b09d-fb7d42c61c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339609} {:answer/id #uuid "74268e37-1f62-5484-8e2e-289685ee280c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340160} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2ae093-3817-5408-b776-763e241cdd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340245} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d79b4f-4909-5ade-98af-aa79d6111bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c2ae093-3817-5408-b776-763e241cdd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340245} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d782e86-1ff4-5e18-8864-3196f9c2c47d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a57d1c4-bb4c-5c4c-98eb-5c77a7edaff8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73327" :question/problem "![](images/70/70a4f7ef7589f303924f1c48e7b0c26cd2173960847ac660199d11ae144c0afc.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, as shown above. Find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\sqrt{2}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30abb31d-0cea-5c7a-831e-96f5de788129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339554} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6421ba-9fc4-5b06-b42e-97b8e9630769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340085} {:answer/id #uuid "8323ac06-5123-5d38-a2e5-414f42c84379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340307} {:answer/id #uuid "e6899588-5e41-526e-95e9-1b4de17de58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341168} {:answer/id #uuid "ead28d9d-44e7-5693-be9e-8e7172b9ab41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30abb31d-0cea-5c7a-831e-96f5de788129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339554} :answer-field/id #uuid "05af01ca-4de3-5a04-b54e-282cded90f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "091e393b-fab2-5cbe-beb6-54d3099ab9eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73333" :question/problem "![](images/18/1819bad0219f1709a01100daad00beb25a8bbc3688f9fe7c41be53bad8010455.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-0.5$ and $x=0.5$, as shown above. What is the area of the region?" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x{|}_{-0.5}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.5)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.5)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e98f9ac-4a01-5bbe-b14d-e0136dc8a635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340108} {:answer/id #uuid "7b102e44-8332-50cb-9a36-832b649d80e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340230} {:answer/id #uuid "7c07685a-ec3b-5beb-ad8e-cf3840f22cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340242} {:answer/id #uuid "c219ca7c-507f-51c9-802a-7d85664a4eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340855} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1a4e53-010a-5a77-b8eb-5a63c727d93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e98f9ac-4a01-5bbe-b14d-e0136dc8a635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340108} :answer-field/id #uuid "791ab147-ce9e-50e4-86c0-e83adc1db7f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "894941c4-4ec1-5dfe-8658-9b67ea8938f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73350" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+\\frac{1}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{16}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3})+3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =16+3(6-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e77e606-3fbe-52e7-aab0-b4caa65a952e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339394} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1abc9f-f1fd-5412-8a4f-d3cc8ce84141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339400} {:answer/id #uuid "4b46d088-8900-59df-a294-e9c5fdb831c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339803} {:answer/id #uuid "caf8e344-d78b-5248-b740-b9b631236223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340926} {:answer/id #uuid "dbec64d1-1c16-522f-a4e9-422136eadbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f1abc9f-f1fd-5412-8a4f-d3cc8ce84141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339400} :answer-field/id #uuid "934751e0-e49e-590a-9aa0-5f8c7d1fc3cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47640681-1f63-58a5-b2bd-140af66b43bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73361" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48658c86-5fff-5d1e-ad97-ca9615485d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339773} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c23eb-6ff2-5bd1-ab4a-f009409818c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340399} {:answer/id #uuid "a839708e-6d86-5a57-b693-6f0e21e650eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340655} {:answer/id #uuid "ced86a89-239e-50b2-894e-628af504a0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340966} {:answer/id #uuid "daa4acb0-2d88-582f-a718-be7e56ba1265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa4acb0-2d88-582f-a718-be7e56ba1265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341071} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2bfd72-4857-53aa-b6a7-f5d94cbdaa7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40d2e0b8-4256-5b72-ae20-79f2c7d72c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-73391" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a00f442-fb57-50c5-bbd9-1fee169237ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7}-\\frac{1}{49}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339210} {:answer/id #uuid "9e692c9d-8c77-5bad-bb2c-ce6b0b04b196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{49}-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340557} {:answer/id #uuid "d83cb14a-5586-599e-b0e7-7d4efca60710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7}-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341048} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf38642-bc7b-5a13-80f5-5fb4cb506698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{49}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341218} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc80829-1f8f-594c-9696-243eccca8ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a00f442-fb57-50c5-bbd9-1fee169237ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7}-\\frac{1}{49}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339210} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b01ee45-8bb0-54ab-ae6b-948698750e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d49e40b2-2af2-5eab-a104-5eba2983ae5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73393" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x}{7x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $7$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{7x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x}{7(x+\\frac{1}{7})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\int \\frac{x}{(x+\\frac{1}{7})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{7} & 1 & 0 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & 1 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{(x+\\frac{1}{7})} & =1+\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{7})}{(x+\\frac{1}{7})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{7(x+\\frac{1}{7})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{7x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{7x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{7x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{7x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[x-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{7}-\\frac{1}{49}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd377a2-95d6-5fde-b6fb-edd9f6c82895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339404} {:answer/id #uuid "3886b9bb-57fc-5b04-9e14-43198654677d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339636} {:answer/id #uuid "a97ece83-bf8e-5d12-9343-39690fdb4170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340671} {:answer/id #uuid "ba478bc4-e72f-56ec-9e2c-5aa0ce4151f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340796} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3a1f4f-8538-5b6c-a02c-9ceb12a2d036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea3a1f4f-8538-5b6c-a02c-9ceb12a2d036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341196} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d077c61-6966-5590-ac4c-55b42acccc9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76b52539-5773-539b-aa13-33f834965a19" :question/math-academy-id "q-73432" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{4}+9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+9.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 9 & 25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{({x}^{4}+9)}\\,{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a79382-4e7c-5565-8f8d-651f202629f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339292} {:answer/id #uuid "5502a3b1-d9e4-5a34-9dd4-491eeed2b23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339908} {:answer/id #uuid "7087f43f-c9bf-565d-98ab-1b54c5d110f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340127} {:answer/id #uuid "82ee8f5a-e3ab-507f-9c16-8da87f52b8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340304} {:answer/id #uuid "edc21a13-792e-5848-97d2-4e2185d5c45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ee8f5a-e3ab-507f-9c16-8da87f52b8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340304} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ca444a-b6c3-53c4-8f09-a91cb7eeef34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12e14ad3-9d99-5fee-b75f-bd6aa0861881" :question/math-academy-id "q-73434" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{({x}^{3}+2)}\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "574af7f2-928a-5a00-b135-4916a6dec950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339926} {:answer/id #uuid "987816d8-ddea-5472-bfa6-60df165674be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340500} {:answer/id #uuid "a07504e1-78da-53c1-a071-bf6d07bb482a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340581} {:answer/id #uuid "c71ac88f-b329-53fd-a776-c30ee71c20b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340893} {:answer/id #uuid "c94af7b5-9c00-52e6-891e-6dbf2ca55530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94af7b5-9c00-52e6-891e-6dbf2ca55530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340913} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d65433-3d6d-5565-a566-451d9ef6b0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077baa93-9f9f-5780-986d-77a93ca959f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73437" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}-2arccscx.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $arccsc$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccscx)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-2arccscx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccscx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-2(-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826695b2-7896-554a-b710-615a2ed245c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)arccotx}{x(1+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340302} {:answer/id #uuid "93f0db0d-31f3-5683-a714-07d8975da0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+(1+{x}^{2})arccotx}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340458} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c32a6-bafe-5590-8786-dbaa106a79b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1+{x}^{2}arccotx}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340917} {:answer/id #uuid "cb871cf7-4c3d-5e61-967e-16852dd2a29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+arccotx}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340931} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c4d346-27c8-58ed-bcdb-42b65dcb2294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{arccotx}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f0db0d-31f3-5683-a714-07d8975da0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+(1+{x}^{2})arccotx}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340458} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c91fda9-15a2-5689-ae97-5f52a67803da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fde98232-ad33-5093-b3ee-9574c7c7e230" :question/math-academy-id "q-73447" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{arccotx}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we let $u(x)=arccotx$ and $v(x)=x$, and apply the quotient rule.\n\nWe use the formulae\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot x-arccotx\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}-arccotx}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{x+(1+{x}^{2})arccotx}{1+{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+(1+{x}^{2})arccotx}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba67c5c0-96ba-5252-813f-4cd1114b1c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468028} {:answer/id #uuid "13ba8213-a2f7-566e-b980-36df26232e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ba8213-a2f7-566e-b980-36df26232e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468029} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae902587-9446-5ee5-a8a7-4011193f99b5" :answer-field/key "$[\\frac{1}{2},1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d08d9480-93aa-548c-b362-0d3f72fe027e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468030} {:answer/id #uuid "824165aa-6ef3-50f6-ac4f-7298a713ae60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d08d9480-93aa-548c-b362-0d3f72fe027e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468030} :answer-field/id #uuid "454cc1b0-9c8b-546c-8b12-f617acd8c694" :answer-field/key "$[0,\\frac{1}{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fec715a9-1922-5be7-9528-532f2fa723ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468032} {:answer/id #uuid "1a30d8c0-d20a-5ca4-8699-68e7f2c7d1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec715a9-1922-5be7-9528-532f2fa723ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468032} :answer-field/id #uuid "7770570e-24c3-51a1-8910-85b4d1c8c7d9" :answer-field/key "$[0,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e05c6d3d-29e1-53ab-9c70-1ad2b27199a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73486" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{x})?$   \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$   is closed, and the function is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a7e63a-5e34-5d00-a029-7b7d1dae266f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339163} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5f036e-ae00-5bec-a798-541577f1a585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339673} {:answer/id #uuid "ac79c25c-624e-5c03-b40d-f27d28728e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340702} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d66d24-d042-5488-9135-c0056b1b4edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341021} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fbd17a-7cce-55c3-8770-0555c5585b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6fbd17a-7cce-55c3-8770-0555c5585b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341034} :answer-field/id #uuid "137c1cfe-1762-59a5-bc01-704b012cbeca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95a2f566-eee4-598f-a09c-af5424f126be" :question/math-academy-id "q-73488" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$  for $f(x)=5-2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5-2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8d0cfd-3ff7-53b6-a6fd-bb619fdf50f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339261} {:answer/id #uuid "697a7597-286e-53a9-bc0b-bbff0b1352e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340066} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac9957c-dca0-55c3-89cf-b033a094b6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340370} {:answer/id #uuid "8c78568b-4e62-5fcd-91c1-dd4422bd167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340388} {:answer/id #uuid "f19faf0f-9d18-5173-950b-c15b38fd2b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac9957c-dca0-55c3-89cf-b033a094b6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340370} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8c01fe-919d-5c86-bd39-b1e88ade5460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12591823-d567-5fce-84b2-fe3d2ba9ebd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73500" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$  for $f(x)=2x-5\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-5\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-5(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5107a3c4-3fbf-5337-9f89-e57141f452ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(5-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{(5-x)}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339868} {:answer/id #uuid "949a3a25-4ea0-5db8-ac33-f429809b9743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340462} {:answer/id #uuid "c813050e-2154-5157-bdbf-7a6cc5929db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{(5-x)}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340900} {:answer/id #uuid "da23eb94-e92b-5196-90af-b3e6e60c86f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341063} {:answer/id #uuid "ec969a7a-a331-5b85-ae7e-0cf433062a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec969a7a-a331-5b85-ae7e-0cf433062a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341213} :answer-field/id #uuid "96dc5de0-fcff-5a01-9b87-28b3beb33641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25fdd6ba-691e-5f6c-ab57-fe0cafbab12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73510" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x)-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5-x)}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abf4c72-fdb0-5af9-b5fd-ae0fef31f40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339218} {:answer/id #uuid "3006d583-f16e-5d0e-b844-fb9f46126f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339547} {:answer/id #uuid "58ac14b0-18e4-5654-8906-1f8fbd918eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339935} {:answer/id #uuid "790e0574-3d28-597c-9244-19f4d13a9da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340206} {:answer/id #uuid "e92f8177-dba9-5a06-8301-1ec125054798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e92f8177-dba9-5a06-8301-1ec125054798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341183} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f35a480-d276-5dfc-a868-82f4e34bed3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00a7e7d3-20f3-5e6c-9ee5-254f2e2cf50c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73513" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3({cot}^{2}⁡x+1)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3({cot}^{2}⁡x+1)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+3\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ed3ec8-3905-5f6f-9a2f-2ab7735300df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339631} {:answer/id #uuid "39a3f0e3-caa9-547e-af78-d5299cb72d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339648} {:answer/id #uuid "74fac45e-7687-567e-92e4-c173b3b7bbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340175} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b65dd6-83c1-553c-b33b-ed750172fcaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340673} {:answer/id #uuid "ab841e54-a3a9-54bf-8504-9fbb5b804b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fac45e-7687-567e-92e4-c173b3b7bbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340175} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1bbcec-fcbd-576d-8667-2c78a327af54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "229fa8ac-a324-584e-838d-1dd2b70fd89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73525" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x-{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x+1={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x-{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{2}({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f092e91-4f61-5603-80d5-8a93282c6a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339399} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2c2bab-1eca-5488-9001-c674cb08e279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339967} {:answer/id #uuid "896af8fb-37f5-589c-b238-bbb774191209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340354} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3d592d-784d-5142-ab34-93f237937bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340566} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d18828-18be-5809-990c-92d134b043c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f092e91-4f61-5603-80d5-8a93282c6a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339399} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d3c55f3-ba0b-5134-9af5-27dd30e3aab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "661506d6-91ed-5312-9a5a-a709acc23a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-73539" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x})=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ccfea7-fed5-5b7e-bc09-bcd69091da1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339639} {:answer/id #uuid "60022a80-d927-5a41-9b20-e5576e6438cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339983} {:answer/id #uuid "89a04548-acfb-5947-b2ac-4e7dff064d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340356} {:answer/id #uuid "afb751a0-88c2-502a-8157-5752b308f317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340732} {:answer/id #uuid "b36db622-271e-564e-927d-d6a6763d261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b36db622-271e-564e-927d-d6a6763d261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b2bee7-ee63-5f93-be18-3bd951bd1c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b53097b6-0795-598d-9c41-894871fbb1f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73546" :question/problem "Find the value of ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d345055-9b62-57cb-a650-376c71bdaf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339250} {:answer/id #uuid "1a355ba7-59df-5715-839b-894a85f6b5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339362} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa857a9-298e-5270-9039-2f08d9b01bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340276} {:answer/id #uuid "8640ec44-7aa5-5d0f-99c0-6c9e79dd9b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340331} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cf22d6-469a-5111-9eda-634188852461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9cf22d6-469a-5111-9eda-634188852461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340793} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f28dba-00cd-5409-8f84-135559291063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68873e64-d445-55f6-917a-87964a442029" :question/math-academy-id "q-73548" :question/problem "Find the value of ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ÷\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={1}^{-1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e36dff-223f-5b4e-b1b4-209444ae4750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339475} {:answer/id #uuid "295c3085-d512-5a69-92dc-71d06b0bdf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339493} {:answer/id #uuid "83e36855-c02f-53e9-947d-e5e2682c194d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340315} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3f0e3a-443b-558d-9213-98e89a32d936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340527} {:answer/id #uuid "daea0c65-3b56-5a21-bd69-1ef30a29e1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e36dff-223f-5b4e-b1b4-209444ae4750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339475} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0dec351-1e40-54db-9221-0fdc8d5c0c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f9ea65f-55fc-5861-a64a-a45dd398be77" :question/math-academy-id "q-73578" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}(-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c0dc97-1bfa-5226-9e8a-83188a4da218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339328} {:answer/id #uuid "3785646e-da4d-5ce4-884b-7241e989b715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339627} {:answer/id #uuid "6b813e4f-15f3-5ca2-a873-e38985156d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340080} {:answer/id #uuid "7c522d09-2793-56be-8f5f-51c906dc7f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340247} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce6c823-b30e-5a3f-ac0b-98f3964eefdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c522d09-2793-56be-8f5f-51c906dc7f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340247} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc74d58b-4531-5b1b-ba07-fd90641c83fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69cbca99-1555-5b49-9e0d-f5bfd292b88d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73601" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{-1}}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{3}^{x}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{e}^{-1}}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{3}^{x}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{x}[{\\int }_{{e}^{-1}}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{-1}}^{{e}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{x}(3+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{[\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{{3}^{1}-{3}^{0}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a99ac4-61b4-5a56-b3ac-d93de9f9dd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339144} {:answer/id #uuid "4904f1fc-dc77-524c-af2d-813ee70dcc8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339783} {:answer/id #uuid "a8efa69b-78be-5a02-81b6-baa9e8fedd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340664} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd060fa-0631-5438-80c1-f6c14e340129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340956} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c989e2-03ee-581f-83f6-e95b613b2103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4904f1fc-dc77-524c-af2d-813ee70dcc8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339783} :answer-field/id #uuid "013f1fb3-558e-5ad1-b4b7-34a42443c53c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65bcf244-fd2b-540c-b6fb-6b4c12786b6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73619" :question/problem "![](images/86/8637901efc126236b190e7012cf65a06be078cb21c3093eff39b927493000138.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1.5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{2}})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{9}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba9b513-fd9e-5532-ac97-f39096b6d80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339229} {:answer/id #uuid "5146b66f-c081-5a5d-8a49-9465f6a29017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339874} {:answer/id #uuid "6f33def5-e388-5e0c-8e27-ef1debc47437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340114} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b7ff34-6e45-5d0f-881a-f864fd3aefad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341276} {:answer/id #uuid "fc039a6e-3ced-5554-821f-11c2bfcbf96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f33def5-e388-5e0c-8e27-ef1debc47437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340114} :answer-field/id #uuid "71a8e5ea-7823-5b4d-bd7c-7ee99e3470da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9fa12f0-8b86-59fd-acb9-b45aae5f88d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73658" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{21}\\frac{1}{x}{log}_{7}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{7}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $21$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{21}\\frac{1}{x}{log}_{7}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{21}{log}_{7}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c85fd39-fb12-5fd0-84ca-1f9410fd86c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{2}^{x}-5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339238} {:answer/id #uuid "35d01e19-8df6-5a98-9a4e-a13ff45e59c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{({2}^{x}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339607} {:answer/id #uuid "ba62682b-6375-50c8-ab0e-ae65ecff8935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{({2}^{x}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340799} {:answer/id #uuid "f040dd33-55b8-577d-b65c-882719ad8257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{{2}^{x}-5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341244} {:answer/id #uuid "fbec05e7-3328-5ebb-a6b8-92e98b6e67b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{({2}^{x}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba62682b-6375-50c8-ab0e-ae65ecff8935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{({2}^{x}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340799} :answer-field/id #uuid "9475f005-9702-55df-8eb5-e3d9087d1fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d8e2bdb-04c0-5bb8-8174-6e672a971ff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73669" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int {2}^{x+1}\\sqrt{{2}^{x}-5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u={2}^{x}-5.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}u={2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {2}^{x+1}\\sqrt{{2}^{x}-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\cdot {2}^{x}\\sqrt{{2}^{x}-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\sqrt{{2}^{x}-5}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {u}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{({2}^{x}-5)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2383613a-52a1-5d64-8c26-ecd04de30f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339435} {:answer/id #uuid "4c648079-c85a-5f5e-8976-d0479a4da8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339821} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5550ab-a530-5982-a558-83f8aec51f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340078} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200317-7a2d-5a1a-9a82-89183fc7851c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340244} {:answer/id #uuid "ec37b5d7-c2b9-52a3-ad41-1f64c1e8cd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec37b5d7-c2b9-52a3-ad41-1f64c1e8cd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341210} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba24916a-286f-51a8-ad99-99bcf753abe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "279b83c3-9e48-526a-b453-9a5fa23b12aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73712" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b8753a-d28b-5470-8f73-93050c06c08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339465} {:answer/id #uuid "6b85678e-6139-5d36-af17-0b94102122cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340081} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fa1f1a-bb6b-520e-bae1-d824104a01c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340605} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8d3b36-56c7-55f4-abd1-ab704350e9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340693} {:answer/id #uuid "fb91a9c0-e0c4-502a-ae2f-5e69dfe1bc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b85678e-6139-5d36-af17-0b94102122cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340081} :answer-field/id #uuid "1611e707-c42e-5085-bbf5-4935f8f58d88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93802a48-b09b-52e0-870e-185ec5edc9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73714" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{36\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/36)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/36}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (6\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24393bc5-cc65-5e3e-98d5-bdf42d918ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339443} {:answer/id #uuid "30b246de-242f-5409-a5b0-f469ba1027d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339555} {:answer/id #uuid "35d2bc95-71a2-5fc7-a101-f077b2a9529a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339608} {:answer/id #uuid "96c93863-ac3c-584e-9322-9f6459bd4e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340488} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7d4bb5-9f5b-5845-81de-98e1414b3d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24393bc5-cc65-5e3e-98d5-bdf42d918ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339443} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d054be-a958-5c0b-b082-28a19f6e300f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5afdcd66-6368-5354-bd74-a259df30653f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73918" :question/problem "Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+20{x}_{2}-10{x}_{3} &=40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for the system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 20 & -10 & 40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 20 & -10 & 40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 20 & -10 & 40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c99a152a-4432-58e7-8095-7a6594ae2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468088} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1282bf-bb72-590a-82cb-57afddffd291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c99a152a-4432-58e7-8095-7a6594ae2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468088} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf27b0a-4178-50bb-a563-2416eaa07855" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75291599-266c-544e-b704-be929998cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468090} {:answer/id #uuid "cf437d27-edd1-584d-be5b-b58d1bf9be5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf437d27-edd1-584d-be5b-b58d1bf9be5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468091} :answer-field/id #uuid "11bc94d4-2ccf-5ec6-bfea-1ec2f439dda1" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5x-y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90497ddb-e287-5539-b1a1-cd58a7c31e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468092} {:answer/id #uuid "80442660-0495-5466-89ce-25cccd762bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90497ddb-e287-5539-b1a1-cd58a7c31e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468092} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc40e59a-282b-5ffd-8ff6-51c6739169ba" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5-x-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5x-y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c30e3f-d29e-5013-9514-d17c3eee3460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339466} {:answer/id #uuid "376779f8-ab66-595c-9bd0-478fd63c0239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339625} {:answer/id #uuid "447b1d64-6d6b-508d-818e-c81c4fc983e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339733} {:answer/id #uuid "744da144-5af4-5254-8514-317a1d8d743d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340164} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6a59f6-df8c-5517-879f-5849fb7de659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c30e3f-d29e-5013-9514-d17c3eee3460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339466} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede24e4d-4785-5cd1-8533-d9afadca7785" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "504ab9d3-58bf-5555-aee6-cd8fa47fea51" :question/math-academy-id "q-74281" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc56d0d-1fc1-55b0-a858-0f72c1814fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339244} {:answer/id #uuid "222afa10-e65e-535c-b778-59ff5f57e53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339420} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab24c23-c3cd-5916-8d10-f8f9cd41df90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339654} {:answer/id #uuid "5240e2fd-ff1b-54d0-9ace-5be02e2c6a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339885} {:answer/id #uuid "839765cf-3921-5bbd-be4f-c72628e60a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "839765cf-3921-5bbd-be4f-c72628e60a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340311} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e95e6b-57c0-5f5a-83f7-9b72881b2c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ded7813f-7862-5c34-8d67-a2dd0cf18a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-74412" :question/problem "$\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}))\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}))\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ef63a0-c750-5bc2-8bd8-46d803ce1c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7+\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339194} {:answer/id #uuid "2149715e-055c-5838-a5fd-c4ec5e2a6fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339415} {:answer/id #uuid "672d1893-2807-5343-bed6-48541cf459e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340040} {:answer/id #uuid "b6017d08-881d-53ad-b2d3-3b6991a45f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340778} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f3a2af-4efe-52a0-be5d-8a4f036b53dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1f3a2af-4efe-52a0-be5d-8a4f036b53dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340990} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a982e9-7945-5d9b-ade9-b65001a1ecd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8517f4c3-6d9b-58e0-ad68-ee37cb097540" :question/math-academy-id "q-74458" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{12}{15},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{9}{15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{12}{15})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{30}-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eeab515-ce1c-5d67-a33d-e6018d4875fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339844} {:answer/id #uuid "68fe57fc-6cbb-5a54-9f6d-ded078ab77bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340062} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6d474c-d3b2-546f-a966-35128e233e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341095} {:answer/id #uuid "e2487570-b2b9-5673-8895-70f4ade4852e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341132} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0a12c9-9f72-58f6-8668-5bf0ef7965b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\sqrt{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2487570-b2b9-5673-8895-70f4ade4852e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341132} :answer-field/id #uuid "a07b5d66-0e2c-5644-9f1a-a864af5c30fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a02eea4e-b04c-5c03-8b84-ddc1ee505059" :question/math-academy-id "q-74469" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1311ed6e-e83e-5330-b7f0-d636f6a5b2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339297} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcf36fa-2d55-592c-ad8b-78c41d03e9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{2},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339838} {:answer/id #uuid "8c284179-af66-5d8a-8667-9b186f818fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340385} {:answer/id #uuid "c8580996-a9fa-571e-8115-0012666076f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340903} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0c0df3-2853-5f2f-bfd2-8f0456b1bf08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8580996-a9fa-571e-8115-0012666076f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340903} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd4ec4f-2ca7-5bcb-a4ae-66b212822a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c21a9421-0e4c-50cb-88ca-b6a37d78a9d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-74474" :question/problem "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-3).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,2,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0698bb77-dfe7-5e73-bad8-5c473928b63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5},1.571,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339182} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca737a7-1cbd-5f99-aacc-902531ef6b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5},3.141,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339242} {:answer/id #uuid "17845463-35df-5f43-9968-6200646df155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,2.214,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339338} {:answer/id #uuid "50d1cbc0-8fe2-5f41-aed9-77260041cb1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{10},3.141,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339864} {:answer/id #uuid "b625a22d-40cb-54f7-b4bb-a50df0d9511b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0.785,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17845463-35df-5f43-9968-6200646df155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,2.214,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339338} :answer-field/id #uuid "0efa825e-403e-5f8a-ae13-36698e846a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "276bbd82-06db-5cb5-9a75-92624054e2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74479" :question/problem "The point $C$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-3,4,-1).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ,z)$ of $C?$ Express $\\theta $ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-3,4)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-3,4,-1)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-3,4).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $C$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-3,4)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{4}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.927\\,295 \\\\  & =2.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $C$ are $(5,2.214,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3726b4ca-1bbe-5c6b-9e7a-23004ada8de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339623} {:answer/id #uuid "60277a26-dbc5-5fc9-bad4-720a706a6a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339986} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67ee9b-7111-5c33-b0fe-80dfccde79db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340936} {:answer/id #uuid "f359639f-8beb-5f7f-b0c3-7f3ee26c0e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341272} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f68ce8-a6d2-514f-bfbd-8b8110a1161c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f359639f-8beb-5f7f-b0c3-7f3ee26c0e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341272} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d63345-e7b2-55a5-a2ae-48c0e36fa06f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79dcf1b7-6a5b-5991-a94b-2a63325fcdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-74482" :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $P$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d84828-816d-58ba-b0e1-a59783e286bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339321} {:answer/id #uuid "58572345-012a-59c7-8cd5-681da81fb4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339933} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0b6220-6641-5efe-9ee6-d7d6cb37f50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340414} {:answer/id #uuid "94b551ba-c95a-53b7-b718-acf3bc05e883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340464} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b3d572-0f3d-5a33-a807-006c49f04637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b3d572-0f3d-5a33-a807-006c49f04637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340586} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8ecf9f-6708-5e94-a6dd-d1dd642fcf7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15e1b03e-f124-5d2e-b837-d378c1598893" :question/math-academy-id "q-74550" :question/problem "The point $D$ has spherical coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $D$ are $(-1,0,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a39b4d-5781-5124-945a-d3c283bf0061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},0,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339448} {:answer/id #uuid "33070bd4-0eec-5638-a89d-8df6aac20c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339580} {:answer/id #uuid "3cec3300-9879-5705-92e0-50fb389c1885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},0,-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339671} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba6e68a-236f-596e-b655-67dae8f3c560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339809} {:answer/id #uuid "b355585e-7922-5937-94df-e81164bde0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},3,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33070bd4-0eec-5638-a89d-8df6aac20c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339580} :answer-field/id #uuid "077b429d-58fb-5dfd-8dd8-9a4b431b6b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9904226e-02ba-53bf-837a-e256f030e7c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74551" :question/problem "The point $B$ has spherical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,\\sqrt{3},3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ec83dc-cb98-54f1-928c-ab31ea02f4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339146} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3a4aeb-fb9e-5fb8-b5ce-ad2dea28f059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339212} {:answer/id #uuid "0d78bb3a-d489-50a0-b7da-d53016953412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339253} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c8e00-6267-5987-9ff6-c554d30b5c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340260} {:answer/id #uuid "b56cf612-e708-53a8-b406-02a953105541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ec83dc-cb98-54f1-928c-ab31ea02f4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339146} :answer-field/id #uuid "a821f4dc-0d73-525f-91a0-3f3dd95b396c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52031823-5a5b-5a93-b5b4-e1f6b24e67f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74554" :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "697be6e0-358e-5c8f-ba3b-a7a6c27cf563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340067} {:answer/id #uuid "89e24342-7b5d-502f-86fe-2b058ccfa5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340359} {:answer/id #uuid "c2778d39-22b4-5b4f-a4d6-39afae3eb47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340858} {:answer/id #uuid "e9709d59-a915-536a-afbe-5fc80b8f4cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341188} {:answer/id #uuid "fb29fa58-f60d-568d-b1ba-c1ac224bb641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb29fa58-f60d-568d-b1ba-c1ac224bb641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341347} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f807fb-df3a-54a9-be7e-64b490aa4336" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2274a373-0a7f-5722-8de2-da35efc5accf" :question/math-academy-id "q-74555" :question/problem "The point $P$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b3b852-a49c-5eeb-92f6-f26e6d188a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ does not lie on $Π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339184} {:answer/id #uuid "191397de-4f1f-5ce1-a2c1-b8de6e1df5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339350} {:answer/id #uuid "459245c0-1823-5440-b561-9af08e968b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339745} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7685b0-ef1b-58de-ad96-cbeee1b70636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340105} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd09f6d-fcdf-5876-9523-8e01ec65ed87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e7685b0-ef1b-58de-ad96-cbeee1b70636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340105} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bebdfbe-9cbd-5971-9a6a-74e16cc92f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcb29558-7233-531f-8a7f-7d0b5b7173b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74602" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,7,-6⟩$, $c=⟨-2,-10,12⟩$, and  $u=⟨2,11,-12⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=2 \\\\ 7s-10t &=11 \\\\ -6s+12t &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -10 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=2 \\\\ 4t &=-3 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t=-3\\,⟹\\,t=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-2t & =2 \\\\ s-2(-\\frac{3}{4}) & =2 \\\\ s+\\frac{3}{2} & =2 \\\\ s & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{3}{4},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{2}b-\\frac{3}{4}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4})=-\\frac{3}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124d51bd-2aae-5ea7-a2b0-cf4311da38dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342400} {:answer/id #uuid "62486216-48d3-5ef2-81a2-192428d9fc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1+2y-5z \\\\ 2y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343072} {:answer/id #uuid "904e183a-7b57-556c-a5d2-101c5bd11a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ -6z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343498} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ea094-25ff-5a40-9f07-fa1d148d8cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y-5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343757} {:answer/id #uuid "c474aa3b-f7ce-508a-8ed6-38110b5498a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124d51bd-2aae-5ea7-a2b0-cf4311da38dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342400} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb4acd0-8375-525d-8189-262b00544043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78ef528f-61bb-5594-b0fa-7d95e275c2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-74613" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x-6y+15z &=-3 \\\\ 6x+12y-30z &=6 \\\\ -6x-12y+30z &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-2y+5z \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3986dfe0-78b8-5298-9c0e-64a7188e7711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21z+4 \\\\ -6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342712} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c052d-b190-5419-b219-90c5040a3dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21z+4 \\\\ 6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343035} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7528ee-1567-5556-81a5-1c2496450e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21z-4 \\\\ 6z+1 \\\\ 2-z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343320} {:answer/id #uuid "9543e1f3-a111-5298-aa95-b9cc07e2fbf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21z+4 \\\\ 6z-1 \\\\ z-2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343542} {:answer/id #uuid "ec48f840-399c-5e84-a62f-187f5ed0f785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21z-4 \\\\ 6z+1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3986dfe0-78b8-5298-9c0e-64a7188e7711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21z+4 \\\\ -6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342712} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a6d968f-8481-567c-9e4d-8dab2310ec5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "920a4776-0058-5c9a-a43b-cd0f1925df4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74629" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-12y+12z &=-4 \\\\ 5x+16y-9z &=4 \\\\ 2x+6y-6z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -21 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-21z &=4 \\\\ y+6z &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=21z+4 \\\\ y &=-6z-1 \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}21z+4 \\\\ -6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a61c80e-2bd7-5903-be6c-7547219b8c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342324} {:answer/id #uuid "31020c78-3c9e-5550-a047-8794a779797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342648} {:answer/id #uuid "bd48ea9f-0ff2-577f-bfb5-2c6be5933f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343903} {:answer/id #uuid "c66637ec-aae0-50da-b862-c86a04d64b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343979} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2bfbd5-a799-5932-95be-864ad71fddf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee2bfbd5-a799-5932-95be-864ad71fddf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344332} :answer-field/id #uuid "20c0d173-6536-55d0-aefd-9951de44df01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9da9be74-20cf-54df-89a0-bc02d03f9903" :question/math-academy-id "q-74633" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =16{(\\frac{2}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d964555-6f83-5191-89db-fd72ffdf14c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438526} {:answer/id #uuid "ad751ae7-e25b-5406-b9e3-27ea4cbed2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d964555-6f83-5191-89db-fd72ffdf14c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1a0230-4005-55bc-8174-e0944a31dd3e" :answer-field/key "The system has no solution" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be3c581-4293-5cdb-b090-f3c480d1c024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438528} {:answer/id #uuid "89e07487-bb39-5368-bf53-e4b20abb5d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89e07487-bb39-5368-bf53-e4b20abb5d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438529} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62a54fe-bb91-5a39-8c45-d4f5ab79b9a1" :answer-field/key "The system is consistent independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d848cbc-71b2-5e07-ad3c-93e27231b7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438530} {:answer/id #uuid "82fdc104-5ebd-5054-99d2-64d287c3ea35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d848cbc-71b2-5e07-ad3c-93e27231b7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438530} :answer-field/id #uuid "a883d2db-1de6-5620-afb1-bd3d06ab629d" :answer-field/key "The system is consistent dependent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0089d19-a4b5-5ec4-beaa-917a294458ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-74637" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=-1 \\\\ x-2y &=1 \\\\ -x-5y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1 \\\\ 7y &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is consistent independent” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1469b54d-ec23-5506-9427-e98021750719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342419} {:answer/id #uuid "927398b8-4695-53ea-8fcb-0952e83514d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343517} {:answer/id #uuid "a8777c97-52fc-5894-b595-71dafedeb50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343719} {:answer/id #uuid "e6549ab5-9ac9-5a3c-930e-da7406fae50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344260} {:answer/id #uuid "ec013d9b-6c06-5d0e-a904-81462767365f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6549ab5-9ac9-5a3c-930e-da7406fae50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344260} :answer-field/id #uuid "668cf6f0-df85-5110-9881-263e6247ddc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353be5b6-f62d-5474-a5e8-6fa7ab3f0134" :question/math-academy-id "q-74715" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3-2\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/0c/0c6548a95b2a81c7dcde33ead78ee0be8d5cee936edc1e2a98c2d59d259404b7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})≈0.588\\,00.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.588\\,00≈3.73\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5a3449-51c7-5e41-af7b-1a13fddb3095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343163} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3ec4ff-a90e-52ee-9e8e-00d008072460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343291} {:answer/id #uuid "7f46bb77-3e6d-5ad5-95d4-4ae5403aa042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343331} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7f0a23-f446-57b3-8a4a-dce9a0081064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343746} {:answer/id #uuid "febb989e-89ce-5841-b742-8292380ade3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f46bb77-3e6d-5ad5-95d4-4ae5403aa042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343331} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b7b234-47df-5611-a686-25c71891a117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50de5bf4-f172-5683-bb63-c927e8858eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-74716" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3-4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eff12ed4bca73dc27f834b0740b5b2235440250e11427f911c8fc29c99fa1d7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <arg⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{4}{3})≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.927\\,30≈4.07\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6d6767-1146-5a58-a3ee-9b876310ebed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343045} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9d5d2f-6bd9-5ff8-8331-4f01157a7195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343314} {:answer/id #uuid "c58fa24c-d4dc-5219-b20d-4ee437fb29e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343973} {:answer/id #uuid "e2159453-cf0d-55e3-93a7-4a03b01ff975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344229} {:answer/id #uuid "f48dd510-8468-5895-a87e-be32fb03d256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c58fa24c-d4dc-5219-b20d-4ee437fb29e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343973} :answer-field/id #uuid "951dc7a2-95ba-5074-8bed-1f1f40a6ebd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cba0666a-04f8-5761-91d5-608acf1e745f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74720" :question/problem "The point $U$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $U$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dccd09c-ad01-5ee8-8083-d1a54808ef20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0.991,6,0.975)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342749} {:answer/id #uuid "516dd9c3-1448-5db0-a4d6-d6ae946815d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,0.199,0.795)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342912} {:answer/id #uuid "bab9c1e7-8cd7-5401-ab1e-554a21dbabde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0.991,0.957)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343878} {:answer/id #uuid "d0df20fa-ae9c-516f-a079-a1866a55bc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0.919,0.975)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344074} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fc8e7a-aba9-5a54-ac8a-3c7712d0cc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{10},0.199,0.759)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "516dd9c3-1448-5db0-a4d6-d6ae946815d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,0.199,0.795)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b398c983-2da0-5518-9ccc-8aee7df3c2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da345849-4daa-55eb-8df7-62d01de63486" :question/math-academy-id "q-74725" :question/problem "The point $S$ has Cartesian coordinates $(7,\\sqrt{2},7).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $S?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+2+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(x,y)=(7,\\sqrt{2})$ lies in the first quadrant,  we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.795.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $S$ are $(10,0.199,0.795).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf412ba-cb44-5810-90eb-470d798d6591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342351} {:answer/id #uuid "38066b67-952e-5aaa-89e3-87ed3e2d7e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342700} {:answer/id #uuid "9e480808-eca1-5207-ab76-299e8586efe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343632} {:answer/id #uuid "d306b071-4ab7-5ed5-b27f-e66587823105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344089} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a03b41-a1b1-5998-84af-ee854f6b5424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6a03b41-a1b1-5998-84af-ee854f6b5424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344121} :answer-field/id #uuid "195ce2b3-5781-555d-8433-34219cc57723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcc4a25a-4ff4-59f4-a945-c409687d5f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-74851" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}})\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨t,4⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+16)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{0}{({0}^{2}+16)\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨0,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0⟩}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c21a3b-7659-540a-8cd1-66aacfac328f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)}(4t\\,i+({t}^{2}-4)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342284} {:answer/id #uuid "1e203976-b8c7-5646-819e-ddf7361f2e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}-4)}(4t\\,i+({t}^{2}+4)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342488} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbc2bda-1b99-5a77-8a53-8fccd2025bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)}(4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+({t}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}})\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343302} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a97ea5-ed25-5946-9bd2-34a05a289191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)}({t}^{2}\\,i+(2t-4)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343677} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bf5f87-91b3-5c17-8100-93ff3894c418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1})}(t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-4)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c21a3b-7659-540a-8cd1-66aacfac328f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)}(4t\\,i+({t}^{2}-4)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342284} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9fc4721-2af1-5eb5-9bdd-94522083e786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3f7825a-5227-5372-94e9-fc80317d8e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74893" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+(t+\\frac{4}{t})\\,j$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{4}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t}\\,i+(1-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}})\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{t})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{{t}^{2}}+{(1-\\frac{8}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{{t}^{4}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{8}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}({t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}+16)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}{({t}^{2}+4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+4)}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)}(\\frac{4}{t}\\,i+(1-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}})\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)}(4t\\,i+({t}^{2}-4)\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09edbcaf-e507-56c2-9601-97a969f32090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442767} {:answer/id #uuid "0c61635a-16c4-5875-a03d-2914bf2b487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442768} {:answer/id #uuid "2decce7e-2100-5b81-a206-46546723c381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442771} {:answer/id #uuid "4143e3f3-49a8-557d-b984-d646890798bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442773} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fbafaa-f05c-5762-8d2f-0ad19854c541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4143e3f3-49a8-557d-b984-d646890798bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442773} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7cace19-2ac2-5d38-9b8e-8da19509dd92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e44b5dc-345c-58d4-a170-3fddfd0702d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75073" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nFind a basis $B$ of  $\\text{Col}(W).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(W),$ we reduce the matrix $W$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f71b28a-eef0-50b5-b4b5-85ae1a337b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342499} {:answer/id #uuid "28b83eaa-9f9a-5888-a2aa-1155dad0a14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{u}_{1},{u}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(U)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342579} {:answer/id #uuid "35d6e4cc-ad73-5db8-bcdb-9c95d86e599c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342681} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8dc9f5-497a-5697-aed3-a18f39d92052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342995} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb60c97-7060-599d-a7c0-7dc790f9f8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f71b28a-eef0-50b5-b4b5-85ae1a337b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342499} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5c1f721-ec7c-5bf5-b0ff-1e3f5a8737f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c9d82d6-6531-5354-9b3b-01e8f3d44990" :question/math-academy-id "q-75083" :question/problem "The matrix $U$ is given below. Let ${u}_{1}$, ${u}_{2}$, and ${u}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $U.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{u}_{1},{u}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(U).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${u}_{2}={x}_{1}{u}_{1}+{x}_{3}{u}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $U$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(U)$ is ${{u}_{1},{u}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${u}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{u}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-2)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{u}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{u}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-2)\\cdot 0=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38d0a784-5fd9-5dda-99ca-883f51f6abc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342706} {:answer/id #uuid "4933b0e6-8fdb-5bb1-8870-15d7f9408f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342840} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0cb831-d862-599e-8e7e-32ee3a3b5666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343184} {:answer/id #uuid "9a57d9c1-5236-5f0d-9b61-6285b27fe930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343596} {:answer/id #uuid "b7bdd1ba-65a0-5e2f-abe2-f90a4069f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4933b0e6-8fdb-5bb1-8870-15d7f9408f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342840} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8a09a2-53d5-50df-85c4-266c0287b22d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52e03100-7b68-59b1-97e7-23738a101a16" :question/math-academy-id "q-75198" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is  $\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a06e174-b40e-542a-aaa8-c20f58478d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7)$ and $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342590} {:answer/id #uuid "773cfdb0-6207-5826-94df-13c1f2047faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343273} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c09ae-372e-5ebb-89f3-5d156de42796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343326} {:answer/id #uuid "bad4b9d3-b637-5250-85a3-4ea2d991b3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343880} {:answer/id #uuid "d05ebe96-be0f-540c-960e-86bd11c13d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05ebe96-be0f-540c-960e-86bd11c13d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9e1595-3b42-5743-84aa-8d4592ec3993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1ab1abc-1cc2-5ae2-8881-b425aa0a15ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-75205" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+1⟩,t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+3t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$, the only solution is $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-\\frac{4}{3}) & =⟨{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{3}+1⟩=⟨\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33866f64-1510-59e1-bc70-4f67d31cce9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342673} {:answer/id #uuid "d04bf2fb-fce7-5faa-ae83-6b24753b85f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344067} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ca02f7-e657-5790-8ecc-cc7960736bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344353} {:answer/id #uuid "f39296c6-cbb8-58da-954a-e372ded44acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344381} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e9ec94-68ca-508d-a569-9bbd35ef59f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33866f64-1510-59e1-bc70-4f67d31cce9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342673} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f27771-372d-52a9-ab4c-cf748b229432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0508c3c2-abfc-50ef-9ddd-8886bbc66cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-75295" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=2,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad01848-8741-5cb8-87a5-f6517290d6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342468} {:answer/id #uuid "71c169d6-5705-5a09-9d3e-2b9fa9418633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343220} {:answer/id #uuid "9a17635b-0972-57cb-b31e-499ad5931fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343594} {:answer/id #uuid "c7525d1b-d02f-5b0f-bcd5-f1d6c6826b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343988} {:answer/id #uuid "f91bc710-bf1b-51b9-8455-71a367e23015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad01848-8741-5cb8-87a5-f6517290d6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342468} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5f01ce-9357-53cf-ac88-d236cd72d59a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af6a3602-6598-5ad6-8883-59a5609544bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75302" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4(0),4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86c569-1b23-58e1-8325-4f3e7e75a69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342353} {:answer/id #uuid "225011dd-679b-5c4b-b454-3b0b1271da1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342522} {:answer/id #uuid "60fd3304-972d-5e5d-9fb2-f58c18df414c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343059} {:answer/id #uuid "8b30390b-535a-5fbf-a016-d26514ebf39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343449} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0dcad5-ba23-558d-b9a1-58b76db4a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf0dcad5-ba23-558d-b9a1-58b76db4a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343917} :answer-field/id #uuid "818560be-6c53-5bbb-8884-04841718675f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e872eb2a-80c0-5fe7-b915-1fedf1110b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-75407" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{4(1)}{({1}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{2}{{1}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}(-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k)+\\frac{2}{3}(j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{81}+\\frac{4}{81}+\\frac{16}{81}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{9}\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{2}{3}q(1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i\\cdot \\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j\\cdot j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14477158-11de-5790-b879-bc43c8ca8958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342417} {:answer/id #uuid "2441c22a-b9d8-5652-ae6e-8b99dc23bcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342545} {:answer/id #uuid "364edc20-5721-5b95-b18f-7e2fcb0c7c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342686} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5c501c-a1fa-5e2e-8a3d-da88a08e2ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343745} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfae4a4-bf96-5ad0-813a-3a5b34415980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdfae4a4-bf96-5ad0-813a-3a5b34415980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343911} :answer-field/id #uuid "49407303-e12b-5aac-a9e2-6cef8ca783a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a57d2de-dfa3-5922-be9d-d7417adc9dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75448" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c81bfce-9632-5e30-aca0-66bfd54ed5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342869} {:answer/id #uuid "6696bf37-f7ff-50df-9318-a659fdf44683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343107} {:answer/id #uuid "669b4061-b6a6-5c32-bcc9-77f78daffdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343108} {:answer/id #uuid "991af7f3-b4f6-515e-a7e8-d7d5a51f6ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343584} {:answer/id #uuid "c959123c-a4c9-5b03-b0d7-7a0e84a3f053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669b4061-b6a6-5c32-bcc9-77f78daffdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343108} :answer-field/id #uuid "619bf93e-94cf-5c5d-8b42-901d98684e52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c985bafb-2593-5b73-b834-81a6aec3295e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75458" :question/problem "Find ${F}^{′}(e)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{a}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{u}{2+{u}^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2x+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(e).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{2e+e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2e+e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "375ef518-4d6a-56b5-8701-cd9802686411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342695} {:answer/id #uuid "41e5f4d8-856b-52f5-85b3-07ac865cef2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342784} {:answer/id #uuid "69d5fca1-eec8-5d54-b1fc-d0f9751e2efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343141} {:answer/id #uuid "745b8d3b-d175-5c42-a346-9db9b829c2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343245} {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779} :answer-field/id #uuid "74402250-c07b-5643-a659-e21ce9c35c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15f4c73-c376-5fbd-b671-915cb2fd0f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75491" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c234f6-092e-5f46-865a-425945404213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343206} {:answer/id #uuid "72377fdc-c5c4-5cad-be35-e357b4b262ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343227} {:answer/id #uuid "8be39f08-c60d-56cb-867b-ae591af34e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343461} {:answer/id #uuid "9f960098-4e3f-597c-a84f-8d0dfb1daa4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343646} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ce6f6-003a-5a0f-82e6-f33b9254bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72377fdc-c5c4-5cad-be35-e357b4b262ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343227} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1ca2441-2869-5b83-a4db-5247f47eb190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a9495b4-c709-53eb-875a-846142be09cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-75521" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j]\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)=1\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j=\\,i+j, \\\\ r(-1)=(-1)\\,i+\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\,j=-\\,i-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0191e885-4eaa-5810-988a-c30eb71a3ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342262} {:answer/id #uuid "056a40fc-c58d-5c4c-ab62-664c240b4261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342290} {:answer/id #uuid "33643afa-ac2e-5b9f-adc3-b2783e20d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342671} {:answer/id #uuid "35947d12-1855-548c-b110-dedcae0828e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342679} {:answer/id #uuid "83eb6ca0-8566-5ea4-a7c4-84d928c8be41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33643afa-ac2e-5b9f-adc3-b2783e20d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342671} :answer-field/id #uuid "b899eb10-9d4b-51ba-91ba-e034d3dbe1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ed3b4a4-4334-5910-b956-ca1595cafddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-75524" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,t\\in [0,\\pi )$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in [0,\\pi )$, the only solutions are $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0\\,,\\,3⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(0,3)$ and $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e4e41-3877-590f-b9fb-f9441f5491c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342541} {:answer/id #uuid "2a868b11-a8ff-5adf-ab9a-9bf43adf6226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342591} {:answer/id #uuid "8689e7c8-b5a6-587c-8850-666b874ed56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396} {:answer/id #uuid "d624b7cc-f612-5da1-90f6-60de0f22b6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344113} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7baec1-72af-559b-b4c9-4860c48320be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8689e7c8-b5a6-587c-8850-666b874ed56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396} :answer-field/id #uuid "db324572-0ff6-5834-9630-8030d14c66f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36f60655-da6b-51ba-a53b-de029dead882" :question/math-academy-id "q-75828" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703429e4-f6ee-5ae0-b54b-621a3b3aa87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343202} {:answer/id #uuid "a374971b-c760-5d83-adbf-3b1936e47374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343673} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd32382-778d-546c-b434-bc2f6f15182c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343889} {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} {:answer/id #uuid "f93e23d5-adfb-539c-932c-d93d3f7f5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e62a385-bb95-5754-89d7-c5b01db0db60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "263b5643-f9a7-58b5-b4f2-be4d8aef81b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75862" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y &=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y &=2 && |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y &=1 && |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=6 \\\\ 2x-y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=6 &&  \\\\ 2x-y &=4 && |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=6 \\\\ -2x+y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2685fdf5-b2ef-5419-a715-1c74ce9389ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342565} {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} {:answer/id #uuid "75251e7a-9236-5e4c-9b53-7cfd86320732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343251} {:answer/id #uuid "978d082a-57ab-588e-8901-810af078103c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343568} {:answer/id #uuid "9f71a6a2-9c4d-582e-afe9-1afe12b68ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4171e3f8-5f6b-53b2-9b4c-3f49129ae323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8936d7-9acb-5f42-aac4-919b57567005" :question/math-academy-id "q-75925" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "291c8e4e-9d45-5531-b4d1-293184452bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342584} {:answer/id #uuid "86c25b24-aec5-5597-aa76-dbce75d2dbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343400} {:answer/id #uuid "9606c201-763a-5f66-8e5e-0d490263e373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343550} {:answer/id #uuid "c49f4552-deab-57bc-bafc-6979cd09a794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343968} {:answer/id #uuid "d6136878-73ad-5b7b-992b-be1d04476b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49f4552-deab-57bc-bafc-6979cd09a794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343968} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21d17cd-7a08-5ec9-a91a-29252aa6e304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18e01818-579e-59cf-ab50-d0abd274fe70" :question/math-academy-id "q-75930" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+3}{3a}=\\frac{a-1}{a}+2.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a$ and $3a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,3a)=3a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+3}{3a} & =\\frac{a-1}{a}+2 \\\\ 3a\\cdot (\\frac{a+3}{3a}) & =3a\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{a}+2) \\\\ 3a\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{3a} & =3a\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{a}+3a\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{\\cancel{3a}} & =3\\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{a}}+6a \\\\ a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a \\\\ a+3 & =9a-3 \\\\ 9a-a & =3+3 \\\\ 8a & =6 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. But since $a=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a7452c-e3f9-526d-82be-805b605b288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342420} {:answer/id #uuid "2377a0c3-c1bd-53c7-93bf-77cbc38fa76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342532} {:answer/id #uuid "7c304e6a-4939-558c-9504-44c7f57d0bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343307} {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} {:answer/id #uuid "cef19a74-15fe-5db4-b7f1-7bf698a07ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9331d1-6e4e-5569-b803-cfc624eda5cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64167e5a-a3e2-55d1-956c-8945cc13aa71" :question/math-academy-id "q-75941" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}=\\frac{m+4}{15m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,5m,$ and $15m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,5m,15m)=15m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $15m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m} & =\\frac{m+4}{15m} \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+3\\cdot 5m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{\\cancel{3m}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{5m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{\\cancel{5m}}) & =\\cancel{15m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{\\cancel{15m}}) \\\\ 5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4 \\\\ 10m-15+3m+15 & =m+4 \\\\ 13m & =m+4 \\\\ 12m & =4 \\\\ m & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d0aa0d-d15b-53b1-b587-29d21d242662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343134} {:answer/id #uuid "8454df55-5625-586c-8f0c-e59a199cf2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343370} {:answer/id #uuid "897525de-2820-5f27-9f87-c7818129d1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343431} {:answer/id #uuid "ace01dcb-3f03-5590-92b8-6bbe03249e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343761} {:answer/id #uuid "c4178081-bdf7-5733-b5be-9444a4cfd6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68d0aa0d-d15b-53b1-b587-29d21d242662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343134} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf34ce4-972f-542d-98a2-41b9843bb493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29f3d15c-3699-5650-acaa-cf842bca8086" :question/math-academy-id "q-75944" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${d}_{3}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $D$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{8}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{3}}=\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3214ac66-089e-5f1a-b5f5-27127d89af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6f8a38-0a5a-5b8d-9bbd-8d6b55a7767b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "69df0ecf-34a7-5ab2-a85a-47fd55e5f70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9fef81-c774-530d-bce3-3ec15ebb79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344469} {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab78a2e2-73c6-516c-bb41-33e676435ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c192211-a062-5d76-8e3f-cac4eb8e50eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342476} {:answer/id #uuid "2b04914a-76f1-51a8-a7c9-b2a4d26db2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342597} {:answer/id #uuid "b72eebd1-ed24-5a3c-9407-3e4f89404335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343845} {:answer/id #uuid "cd05bdbf-6440-5186-86f9-6868a4471153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344045} {:answer/id #uuid "d38031bb-76d7-5bef-b74e-24bfabbc3646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd05bdbf-6440-5186-86f9-6868a4471153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344045} :answer-field/id #uuid "96deaadc-3e11-5c45-b3ef-17e9f80f5c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a0673ad-ec47-547a-a169-9262732d993a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75987" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23632137-2307-53ed-97ca-6d194aedb4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342531} {:answer/id #uuid "37d31442-bc77-52e8-8b42-ed69f127f1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342699} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba06784-f60e-51b7-a033-405a8dce8d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498