ro:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}>0\\,⟹\\,x-2>0\\,⟹\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}})$ is $(2,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8011c1-9cfc-5fa1-bbdd-091c67e18332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342719} {:answer/id #uuid "63cef5b5-d373-5b1f-8528-cc13e3026246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343086} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc3db4c-1ba1-5e07-a179-70559ebd7afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343169} {:answer/id #uuid "725e4d8f-30f3-541a-b361-47b9d40fc3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343229} {:answer/id #uuid "d7be1e3b-a646-5934-9f54-a7f5ec33461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725e4d8f-30f3-541a-b361-47b9d40fc3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b10f5e9-21ca-540c-a98f-057955c52343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c47b28a-2993-50a5-9de3-ebf5581e87c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-76106" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x-1} & =3 \\\\ 2x-1 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 5-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188201ef-f62b-54fe-91ea-ab9262b8bb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342454} {:answer/id #uuid "2012c3e8-acfe-5689-ba9a-41ed1fb940e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342501} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbbdc6b-3da7-534d-ad34-7a670c808ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343180} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f0e26d-cba8-5c20-bd03-964668ddac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343974} {:answer/id #uuid "cb94719a-6026-5bb2-956b-94cdf8043975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb94719a-6026-5bb2-956b-94cdf8043975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344030} :answer-field/id #uuid "9acaee00-2ee9-5648-9ed0-2f7eb59f1242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "373bfa7c-f3cf-5be4-9a31-c2d8cf824718" :question/math-academy-id "q-76255" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 3\\text{i} & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operation:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}}){R}_{1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{+}1 \\\\ 3\\text{i} & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 3\\text{i}+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}(2-\\text{i})}{2-\\text{i}}) & -2+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}(2\\text{i}+1)}{2-\\text{i}})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{(-4+2\\text{i})+(6-3\\text{i})}{2-\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2deb5ecd-9fcb-5de8-a47f-2aac32b50a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342625} {:answer/id #uuid "4f75afc8-c8e2-552b-bf47-67f0c0a13567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342897} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd46a16-dd73-5cd7-848b-a231cc8b9c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343050} {:answer/id #uuid "6734105b-9de9-5be8-a53a-4158d98ddacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343113} {:answer/id #uuid "f715325d-e759-56c1-bf0d-6cd6e0eee098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},-2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd46a16-dd73-5cd7-848b-a231cc8b9c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343050} :answer-field/id #uuid "58052dca-b02a-538c-bcb6-2bc7ad90fc13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9155ce6a-a592-5aef-9c65-bf69cca03a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76369" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}-4y=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}-4y & =-2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =-2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4b4a86-f169-5c4f-87f5-d0b62ec60298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342323} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce40828-670c-5edd-87b2-c0cb375a82f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342739} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed3b169-f0d4-5213-afa6-342dddae141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342758} {:answer/id #uuid "92eceef3-679c-5de1-b434-dd837f79c2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343524} {:answer/id #uuid "d14e25aa-c14c-530e-b787-28f9bb6a6b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ed3b169-f0d4-5213-afa6-342dddae141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342758} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2b3b34-415d-5dcc-bc53-182c2750c2e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "385da02b-d681-50b2-a172-67bb1e6a6af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-76370" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+3x+{y}^{2}-y=-\\frac{9}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+{y}^{2}-y & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+3x)+({y}^{2}-y) & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{9}{4}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-(-\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{1}{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372a5048-17c2-5076-9635-4ff4ad531da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342693} {:answer/id #uuid "60710012-7ccb-54d9-aac8-7c21a4db39de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343056} {:answer/id #uuid "7143c54a-f0e9-502c-b646-975daaf14a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343209} {:answer/id #uuid "77d755de-6003-5532-a85f-2d9e8cb137d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}-2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343274} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c1d1a-5321-50f7-aff8-7b81e9c745ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60710012-7ccb-54d9-aac8-7c21a4db39de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343056} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d47b24e-5186-5a2c-bc62-0a4991b4b99e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e477990a-d15e-595b-a078-39c7382f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-76377" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2},$ $y=t-1,$ and $z=t+1.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({t}^{2})-(t-1)^{2}-(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-({t}^{2}-2t+1)-({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}+2t-1-{t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2(2{t}^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b986f99-dbf2-5721-9c5f-e28e10f22836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342338} {:answer/id #uuid "8b32fc34-f436-5a96-bd40-dfbb2b02b7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343451} {:answer/id #uuid "986babd3-8642-5f38-b06b-f77cd25a6e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343576} {:answer/id #uuid "c61d6ac5-d343-5721-8ec3-ef2a1c75053a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343976} {:answer/id #uuid "cebfa446-9404-5449-ade5-0f39864d83f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986babd3-8642-5f38-b06b-f77cd25a6e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343576} :answer-field/id #uuid "6acd1e7a-f9b0-54b4-b6b0-e3a07466b614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4796dc75-cdb4-53f9-8768-a4a7b1757c4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76401" :question/problem "Express $3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n3({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df8c281-7720-5de4-bfcb-7ec3f40cd92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+2){e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342878} {:answer/id #uuid "8964b90d-0fb7-5352-a378-5854b735da72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{5t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343429} {:answer/id #uuid "90b58700-1443-53b7-900c-fe295b8988e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343503} {:answer/id #uuid "92c59c13-5a91-5d60-9e87-defff709bc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343520} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e0ad1c-9242-5e5e-8095-61f3d7da1e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\sqrt{26t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b58700-1443-53b7-900c-fe295b8988e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343503} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4fe4ae-1db5-5267-909a-483c3e60cca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d30373c-bec9-57f2-8b55-adb3bc1e9678" :question/math-academy-id "q-76414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=5x+2$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,5t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+2)j \\\\  & =i+5\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5t+2){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} {:answer/id #uuid "21e4ce2b-359a-5d4a-b474-cf8acda842ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342519} {:answer/id #uuid "3109d7d4-9f83-52e3-8ef2-13bdd11c2459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342650} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d1ba27-795d-5ca7-bff5-0a3d75d5b66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343667} {:answer/id #uuid "d10bbab9-4de9-5d18-a90a-7d251ca7ad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec0e9-f2ac-56af-9dc2-ed440ea409ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2497006d-58cf-53a4-9f8c-593f6d881048" :question/math-academy-id "q-76431" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2157cbec-dd3c-50c6-8f66-e922ba1f66ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342514} {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b6665a-0ccb-5b28-87e4-9cb8ee2700ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi -1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342928} {:answer/id #uuid "a88c1662-aadb-5062-a115-55611ab6c159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343722} {:answer/id #uuid "bfef22ae-f97c-5172-89a1-1bc5619b881d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} :answer-field/id #uuid "09fe84d2-3042-5159-a1de-85adb6692d1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5649582e-ddb6-5db4-a7c3-699a007eaafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-76433" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7aeab2-63a6-55b6-9670-af11f7878d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342743} {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} {:answer/id #uuid "7df5078f-cc9c-5640-bdbe-c73ad5edaf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343323} {:answer/id #uuid "d44736e7-181c-5708-8fc6-091f82bf604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344100} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc86749-0a58-547d-b292-dd0c15c33cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbabc5e-406f-59f6-a3fe-8b87b2e1d083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a51cb6-cac0-57a7-bccc-fcb3f00d4af4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76434" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=t-4.$     \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(4-t)}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}t\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec7f114-71dd-5707-a837-49ec4e0f58d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343037} {:answer/id #uuid "784dc8af-a9a4-53e0-8947-d481e7980aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af1a3-65b7-5d58-b8b9-b68cc63abf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343333} {:answer/id #uuid "e3af6d11-71f3-548a-854f-ff88ce98cd06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b827e50e-d12a-5657-bed2-ba45d90ca8cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6c30ef5-4b00-5501-a5b6-18892cf4af06" :question/math-academy-id "q-76447" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4{t}^{2}$ and $y=3{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =8t\\,i+6t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =10|t| \\\\  & =10t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2{y}^{3}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(4{t}^{2})}^{3}-2{(3{t}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}{t}^{6}-2\\cdot {3}^{3}{t}^{6} \\\\  & =64{t}^{6}-54{t}^{6} \\\\  & =10{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{6}\\cdot 10t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =100{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{8}}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =100[\\frac{{1}^{8}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{8}}{8}] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec86e50-8126-5c8c-9c9e-7b518ae7992e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342364} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8f2e2d-51c7-5375-9e3c-dedb16da721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343311} {:answer/id #uuid "a735d400-c4c3-527f-96ac-bc42ae20a5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343710} {:answer/id #uuid "adb27abf-79df-56ff-bf2b-b4946023e074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343766} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75af4f4-1064-571f-8598-a6fa76be3720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d73cf87-1a19-5cca-88b7-764818d04cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76479" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0<t<\\sqrt{\\pi /2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,j-2t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872230-dffa-5440-9150-e6a7484563c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342745} {:answer/id #uuid "41027de0-afa8-5c2b-8896-f3199a916c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342773} {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} {:answer/id #uuid "970fe583-040b-5004-90dd-58e93faa89d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343562} {:answer/id #uuid "bad2408b-9fca-5a3a-8105-a9d53cef466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5d8aa7-e05a-504e-8119-e3b3b13e8c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d9906db-a501-54d2-bbd1-292e69086032" :question/math-academy-id "q-76480" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3{x}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}$, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{3/2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4b14bf-da94-5587-9476-c6c9f1763513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342466} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd66d9f-895b-589b-a593-4fcc58493314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342486} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d5513-4913-5c07-b97e-b502cd923def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342600} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5cec4d-eafc-5470-8930-282ed398fa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e475247-06f5-5122-b5e7-edfc19026889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ffd2389-f864-5810-9cd3-9a41b1f9dc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-76484" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5},$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$   \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =5|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t| \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =25{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =25\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6045acb1-8b9a-5676-9cd0-f476e917695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343055} {:answer/id #uuid "78f375e1-b70a-5ed9-91e3-487c68d7b979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343283} {:answer/id #uuid "9a216f95-d7dd-56ff-a663-b17e7986e503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343595} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ff0d93-7c8a-5709-b129-b090117e05ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344250} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a2e8b-d768-5657-967c-eae45b12f823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78f375e1-b70a-5ed9-91e3-487c68d7b979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343283} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a04b20-97d2-5960-810a-402a92da8b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc662357-bb5a-5c14-b4f2-075042070ad7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76488" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={150}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${150}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={150}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/d6/d61c6dbd94be14f2a455791a584a00f19ab7b52452b3acda984c5b278a5e39ed.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=150\\cdot 210=31\\,500.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b458ab9-f8d2-5847-a1d7-a82898766471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342334} {:answer/id #uuid "20197b9b-a175-536e-b070-6b0342f0432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342502} {:answer/id #uuid "4e63fcf2-15c2-57a2-9183-f2e3d764402c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342883} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5024a8-ad7b-5afe-b439-92410b5aeb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343012} {:answer/id #uuid "7639ae17-8091-53b1-8ba7-0966fa92e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b458ab9-f8d2-5847-a1d7-a82898766471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342334} :answer-field/id #uuid "78baf57b-c872-5926-9e6f-da7c5cccc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20b45040-332b-5b0d-9817-efef67df2755" :question/math-academy-id "q-76490" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=1.403\\,348…≈1.403\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.403\\,3$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.403\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b3d60be42ec916f0e62f3a86cb1ce2aa628482c97f68f0df7c6ab533b919102.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.403\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.879\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.403\\,3\\,\\cdot 4.879\\,9≈6.8\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b563221-3548-560c-9fc8-b290090582fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342472} {:answer/id #uuid "34a3d682-f094-5b8e-9966-be8b9fc8ad8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342675} {:answer/id #uuid "618e4548-1271-521c-9a14-4e3337de95f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343065} {:answer/id #uuid "cd843b1d-6720-54f4-98b0-8e4c42afe883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344048} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf920228-2dc2-55b3-a8f5-efbc9659d941" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "004b67de-a618-52fc-b8e0-1a45d41d2be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76496" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "629598a2-bf76-5692-87df-54a7aebab167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343074} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac07520-bae0-5d27-a68e-d13a143ff9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343446} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfbb60-3559-5add-b48d-7ec204e4d217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343767} {:answer/id #uuid "be04fed9-e037-5f1b-9c74-a5caaef8e7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343912} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bf0071-88d4-5078-a1a8-093a1a23e04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "629598a2-bf76-5692-87df-54a7aebab167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343074} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdbb8fb-f459-54a9-a836-e4514336c164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d978c8fd-7c4e-51e3-a8f6-ce9bb51f0bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-76499" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get the first solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/af/afb20de50d2fa92c6550d1e5357b2aaf07311177e3ed82e1a15facccf2c0f0db.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value does not lie in the required domain. To get the solution that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4c92cd-b35d-5c18-a223-49150a03011a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342724} {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} {:answer/id #uuid "a719e5db-f272-5b05-bc3d-faa57d00c911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343708} {:answer/id #uuid "ac56b14e-40b3-5364-ac94-8f30ec9d2137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343754} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f2338d-321a-5c08-ba22-d59d140ae37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe61a69d-0a4f-5fcc-b1ef-4f5a0dca0429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bfd19a9-a685-5ea5-8c9a-dcf991921379" :question/math-academy-id "q-76564" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+7j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[4\\,i+7j-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(3i+5j) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\,i+(2+5t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+3t$ and $y=2+5t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)j \\\\  & =3i+5j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+3t)(2+5t) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}+11t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{34}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{[5{t}^{3}+\\frac{11}{2}{t}^{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}(5+\\frac{11}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d469bce-94af-5634-ab82-5aa6af354e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342616} {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} {:answer/id #uuid "85c4d835-de5f-5323-ac7e-857e358d872b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343386} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d73c23-a898-521d-b2b3-a61c11d3d91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343697} {:answer/id #uuid "baa7a4d0-7ea9-5d62-9f82-07ada8a4ece3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b45262a-d4fd-5260-bbcc-f503f4fcadd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ad0c20-f0d9-5a56-ac84-89e9373c74a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76569" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from$(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=2\\,i+j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[(2\\,i+j+3\\,k)-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(i-j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j+3t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t,$ $y=2-t,$ and $z=3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)k \\\\  & =i-j+3\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)(2-t)^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}(\\frac{1}{4}-1+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} {:answer/id #uuid "368a2103-e780-5582-ad6f-3a21aa3a8980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342688} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbd128-6385-59fc-8240-9d369f5dd5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343182} {:answer/id #uuid "b163fa1b-99f8-590b-a1f2-94f8b252cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343793} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47b99b-71b1-565f-a23d-6d3b20733bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed33cfa-1f9a-54f9-9d11-cd56d8af78bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07d5db28-c47d-5f6c-be75-b0e883a1921b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76577" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j+k$ and $b=i+2\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t[(i+2\\,j+5\\,k)-(-i+2\\,j+k)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,k) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+2\\,j+(1+4t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-1+2t,$ $y=2,$ and $z=1+4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+0+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}((-1+2t)+(1+4t))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
